# Put PrBoom's .bss in PSRAM.
#
# Installing DOOM cost ~98KB of INTERNAL DRAM whether or not it ran
# (internal_free 127,415 -> 29,035 at the launcher). That is not DOOM's problem
# to carry alone: internal DRAM is the scarce resource on this board and every
# other module pays for it. speed_demon.h says exactly this — static .bss is
# reserved by the linker and is not recovered by unloading modules.
#
# It is diffuse rather than one big array (r_main 17.8K, hu_stuff 11.7K,
# r_plane 10.2K, r_draw 7.0K, d_deh 6.6K, g_game 5.1K ...), so there is nothing
# to relocate by hand. A whole-archive mapping is the right instrument.
#
# Only .bss moves. .data cannot — it needs initialised content copied from
# flash before PSRAM is up — so m_misc's and m_menu's config tables (~27KB
# together) stay internal. Recovery is therefore partial by construction.
#
# This is safe here specifically because nothing in libdoom.a runs before PSRAM
# is initialised: DOOM starts from an app task long after boot. The same
# mapping applied to a driver that touches its statics during early startup
# would fault.
#
# Requires CONFIG_SPIRAM_ALLOW_BSS_SEG_EXTERNAL_MEMORY, already enabled for this
# device (sdkconfig_tdeck_plus.overrides).
#
# The performance question is real and unresolved: PrBoom's renderer state is
# hot, and PSRAM is slower than internal. The framebuffer is the far larger
# consideration though — i_video.c asks for it internal first and falls back to
# PSRAM, and at 29KB free that fallback was guaranteed. Trading diffuse cold-ish
# renderer state for a fast internal framebuffer should be the better bargain,
# but it is a bargain, not a free win, and wants measuring on hardware.

[mapping:doom]
archive: libdoom.a
entries:
    * (extram_bss)
